===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|insignia}}

# A [[patch]] or other object that [[indicate]]s a person's official or [[military]] [[rank]], or [[membership]] in a [[group]] or [[organization]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:badge}}
#* {{quote-web|en|author=Steven Pifer|work=Five years after Crimea’s illegal annexation, the issue is no closer to resolution|url=https://cisac.fsi.stanford.edu/news/five-years-after-crimea’s-illegal-annexation-issue-no-closer-resolution|publisher=The Center for International Security and Cooperation|date=18 March 2019|text=The little green men were clearly professional soldiers by their bearing, carried Russian weapons, and wore Russian combat fatigues, but they had no identifying '''insignia'''. Vladimir Putin originally denied they were Russian soldiers; that April, he confirmed they were.}}
# A [[symbol]] or [[token]] of personal [[power]], [[status]], or [[office]], or of an official body of government or jurisdiction.
#* {{RQ:Mary Shelley Last Man|I|6|passage=The former Protector tendered him the oaths, and presented him with the '''insignia''' of office, performing the ceremonies of installation.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} A mark or token by which anything is known.
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|page=80|volume=III|text=But if autumn wear the '''insignia''' of nature's royalty, its purple and gold, in only the shaded lane or the green field with its one or two old trees, what is its more than eastern pomp in a wooded empire like the New Forest!}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2006|author=P. Goodrich|title=The Laws of Love: A Brief Historical and Practical Manual|page=49|text=Rule 23: “The stress of love makes it hard to eat and sleep.” To these empirical laws we can add the various other '''insignia''' of love.}}

